      "content": "# epub2md\n\nA CLI tool to parse and convert EPUB files to Markdown. Installed via npm.\n\n- **Latest version:** 1.6.3\n- **Install:** `npm install -g epub2md`\n- **Source:** \u003chttps://github.com/uxiew/epub2MD\u003e\n\n---\n\n## Commands\n\n`epub2md` uses flags (not subcommands). The EPUB file path is passed as a positional argument.\n\n| Flag | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| `-c, --convert` | Convert EPUB to Markdown (default command) |\n| `-a, --autocorrect` | Convert EPUB to Markdown with spell-check autocorrect |\n| `-i, --info` | Show basic book metadata (title, author, etc.) |\n| `-s, --structure` | Show the table of contents tree |\n| `-S, --sections` | List all sections/chapters in the EPUB |\n| `-u, --unzip` | Unzip the EPUB file |\n| `-l, --localize` | Download all remote images to a local `images/` folder |\n| `-m, --merge` | Merge all Markdown files into one output file |\n\n---\n\n## Usage\n\n### Basic conversion\n\n```bash\n# Convert an EPUB to Markdown (default)\nepub2md book.epub\n\n# Same as above (explicit flag)\nepub2md --convert book.epub\n```\n\n### With autocorrect\n\n```bash\nepub2md --autocorrect book.epub\n```\n\n### Get book info\n\n```bash\nepub2md --info book.epub\n```\n\n### View table of contents\n\n```bash\nepub2md --structure book.epub\n```\n\n### List sections\n\n```bash\nepub2md --sections book.epub\n```\n\n### Unzip the EPUB\n\n```bash\nepub2md --unzip book.epub\n```\n\n### Merge converted Markdown into a single file\n\n```bash\n# Merge into one file (specify output filename)\nepub2md --convert book.epub --merge=output.md\n```\n\n### Localize remote images\n\n```bash\nepub2md --convert book.epub --localize\n```\n\n### Combine multiple options\n\n```bash\nepub2md --convert book.epub --merge=notes.md --localize\n```\n\n### Batch convert with glob patterns\n\n```bash\nepub2md --convert \"*.epub\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Output\n\n- Conversion creates a directory alongside the EPUB containing the Markdown files.\n- The tool reports progress: number of files created and their names.\n- When `--merge` is used, a single merged Markdown file is produced instead.\n"
    }
